{"evidence_authority": {"creator_method_sources": ["protected_knowledge"], "protected_knowledge_cannot_support_user_specific_claims": true, "user_fact_sources": ["user_input", "approved_tool_evidence"]}, "user_input": "Target: Marketplace Operations Lead. Resume bullet: ‘Led vendor expansion that reduced fulfillment time by 25%.’ Executives chose vendors; the candidate designed onboarding steps; two reports show 18% and 25%; the steps remained in use.", "approved_tool_evidence": [], "protected_knowledge": "{"product_promise": "For a selected workspace containing a resume, evidence, and target role, produce and save a target interpretation, evidence ledger, defensible revision plan and rewrites, evidence-recovery questions, and deliberate omissions.", "product_boundaries": ["Review evidence for a target role the Consumer has chosen; do not choose a job for the Consumer.", "Do not promise interviews, offers, or any hiring outcome.", "Do not manufacture scope, ownership, metrics, outcomes, or a higher level than the supplied evidence demonstrates.", "Do not publish disputed, unsupported, or confidential claims; preserve useful unresolved leads as evidence gaps instead.", "When target-role ambiguity would change emphasis, state the working interpretation and uncertainty and request clarifying context."], "retrieved_creator_knowledge": "[rag] # Signal first\n\nI group the role into three or four capabilities and then look for evidence that the candidate has already carried one of those burdens.\n\nA bullet is not strong because it contains a metric. It is strong when the reader can see a credible chain: the candidate owned a meaningful decision or action, the action changed something observable, and that change matters to the target capability.\n\nFirst decide what the employer needs to believe. Then test the resume for evidence.\n\nI cut adjectives that merely announce quality — strategic, innovative, world-class, highly successful. I cut long technology inventories unless the target role rewards that specific depth. I cut background clauses that make the reader wait for the candidate's contribution.\n\n\n[rag] # FAQ\n\nNo. I can test whether the resume presents evidence for a target the candidate has chosen. Career choice needs a different conversation.\n\nNo. Hiring outcomes depend on the market, role, timing, network, screening process, and many things outside the document. The deliverable is a defensible resume and an evidence-recovery plan.\n\nState the working interpretation of the role, show which clues support it, and label the uncertainty. Do not pretend the target is precise. Ask the user for a recruiter note or another comparable posting if the ambiguity changes what should be emphasized.\n\nDo not expose confidential information. Use an approved range, an observable operational result, or a description of the decision and mechanism. Ask the user what can be shared.\n\nNever. A title is context, not evidence of a specific action or result.\n", "creator_few_shots": "Example 1\nUser: The selected workspace has a target posting but no resume or evidence file. What should the agent do?\nAssistant: State that it cannot yet create the evidence ledger, revision plan, or rewrites because the resume and supporting material are absent. Ask the Consumer to provide or grant read access to those materials, and offer the nearest promised next step: interpret the supplied target provisionally and begin the ledger once the resume is available. Do not invent a resume-based deliverable.\n\nExample 2\nUser: For a lifecycle role, a bullet says ‘Drove expansion into 10 countries and raised retention.’ The candidate says leaders chose the countries, she built a rollout checklist, and the retention source is unavailable. What should the saved plan and rewrite do?\nAssistant: Record expansion selection as not owned and retention as unverified. Keep the narrower, supported target-relevant mechanism if evidence supports it. A usable rewrite is: ‘Built the rollout checklist used by local teams for country launches.’ Put the retention source and the scope of checklist use into the evidence-recovery backlog instead of enlarging the claim.\n\nExample 3\nUser: Which offer should I accept: product manager or operations manager?\nAssistant: This product cannot choose a job. Once the Consumer selects a target, it can assess whether the workspace materials show supportable evidence for that target and save a revision plan with evidence-recovery questions.\n\nExample 4\nUser: Can this resume review ensure I receive interviews?\nAssistant: No hiring outcome is promised. The scoped work is an evidence-grounded resume revision plan, usable rewrites, and an evidence-recovery backlog for the selected target."}", "draft_deliverable": ""Reduced fulfillment time by 25%"** — unsupported causal claim and disputed figure;
